Keep the Bionic FORTIFY bridge opaque to LTO and to builtin folding.

Backport of the upstream fix that landed after the 1.0.3 tag.

src/compat/bionic_abi_exports.cc deliberately defines __memcpy_chk, __strcpy_chk,
__vsnprintf_chk and friends, which collide with glibc's own entry points.  Under
whole-program LTO GCC can fold a fortified host call back into Mocktail's own
wrapper, and the resulting tail recursion never terminates.  -fno-builtin matters
even without LTO, because Gentoo's GCC turns on _FORTIFY_SOURCE at -O2 and would
otherwise be free to rewrite the wrapper's plain memcpy() call back into
__memcpy_chk().

Not needed by mocktail-9999, which already carries this upstream.

--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -525,6 +525,19 @@
   PRIVATE src
 )
 mocktail_apply_compile_options(mocktail_bionic_abi_exports)
+# These exported Bionic FORTIFY symbols intentionally overlap glibc's
+# __*_chk entry points.  When the bridge is visible to whole-program LTO,
+# GCC can fold a fortified host call back into its own compatibility wrapper
+# and turn the resulting tail recursion into an infinite loop.  Keep this
+# translation unit opaque to interprocedural optimization and make its calls
+# to the host C library explicit external calls.
+if(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID MATCHES "GNU|Clang")
+  set_property(SOURCE src/compat/bionic_abi_exports.cc APPEND PROPERTY
+    COMPILE_OPTIONS
+      -fno-lto
+      -fno-builtin
+  )
+endif()
 target_link_libraries(mocktail_bionic_abi_exports PRIVATE
   mocktail_bionic_stdio_runtime
   mocktail_window
